 "conclusion": "Subsampling full to k criteria with no compilation of any kind gives a sham curve over k=1..15 at 60 draws each. At core's own median criterion count k=4 the random subsets average 0.42626 with a 95% band [0.41999,0.43530]; core scores 0.33960, z=-21.32. Full scores 0.40590, so core's advantage over full is +0.06630 while a RANDOM 4-subset's advantage over full is -0.02036. Conditioning on the per-pair score gap collapses core's advantage from +0.06630 to +0.06683, and as a share of headroom from 16.3% to 24.4%. full decides 31.0% of its pairs on a gap below 0.05 against core's 19.5%. Under the three tie policies the advantage is exclude=+0.0663, half=+0.0663, error=+0.0661. WORLD: W-COMPILATION-REAL. core beats random subsets of its own size, so compilation contributes something a count-matched sham does not, and the founding number survives with its mechanism named.",
